## Building Digital Dice and Coins

`math.random_range(min, max)` picks a random integer between `min` and `max` (inclusive). Wrap it in your own functions for clean, reusable random tools.

### Dice Roller

```xiom
fn roll_d6() -> Int {
  return math.random_range(1, 6);
}

fn roll_d20() -> Int {
  return math.random_range(1, 20);
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(roll_d6());   // 1-6
  io.println(roll_d20());  // 1-20
  return 0;
}
```

Run it multiple times — the numbers change! Each call to `math.random_range` produces a fresh random value.

### Coin Flipper

```xiom
fn flip_coin() -> Str {
  let result = math.random_range(0, 1);
  if result == 0 {
    return "Heads";
  } else {
    return "Tails";
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(flip_coin());
  io.println(flip_coin());
  io.println(flip_coin());
  return 0;
}
```

### Simulating Multiple Rolls

```xiom
fn roll_two_dice() -> Int {
  let die1 = roll_d6();
  let die2 = roll_d6();
  return die1 + die2;
}

fn roll_until_six() {
  let mut rolls = 0;
  let mut result = 0;
  while result != 6 {
    result = roll_d6();
    rolls = rolls + 1;
  }
  io.println("Took " + " rolls to get a 6!");
  io.println(rolls);
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(roll_two_dice());
  return 0;
}
```

### Why Wrap Random in Functions?

`flip_coin()` is much clearer than `math.random_range(0, 1)` with a manual if/else. Your function name tells the story. Plus, if you want to change how the coin works (weighted coin, anyone?), you change ONE function.

